  {
    "id": 2900,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "This sentence just pops in with no introduction. How do we, as the reader, come from the previous sentence to this one? E.g. \"While the proposed methods like a, b, and c show potential, they do not fully eliminate the hallucination. One approach not yet explored is generative fusion ....\"\n",
    "annotationId": 2852,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2901,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "If you use \"such an\" the following wording should have already appeared in the text before, which it does not here",
    "annotationId": 2853,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2902,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "Research questions should not be answerable with yes or no.",
    "annotationId": 2854,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2903,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "This requires LLM-Blender to work somewhat in the domain, which we do not know yet. If we know it, the previous section failed to mention that it works in this context.",
    "annotationId": 2855,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2904,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "These are somewhat follow-up questions and less sub-questions. Sub-questions would only consider parts of RQ, but they open new explorations.",
    "annotationId": 2856,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2905,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "To my knowledge, this is false. Or is it? I don't know I didn't invest much time in looking for alternatives. Such phrases express that you are unsure about your own work and try to be as vague as possible to always say \"we didn't say it that way\". Either you know it or you don't.",
    "annotationId": 2857,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2906,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "why later?",
    "annotationId": 2858,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2907,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "You state k as 3 and being very precise while you state \"a scoring mechanism\". Assigning random numbers is also a scoring mechanism. Not a good one but still one",
    "annotationId": 2859,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2908,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "You? Or other authors?",
    "annotationId": 2860,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2909,
    "user": "Stephanie Morgan",
    "role": "expert",
    "text": "You don't try to do something. You do it. You are not done until it is optimized or have an answer why it can't be done",
    "annotationId": 2861,
    "parentCommendId": null,
    "tags": [],
    "review": 2,
    "votes": [],
    "group": 2
  },
  {
    "id": 2910,
